s tile [PATH] from the selected ruleset\\n- dictionary configuration remains explicit and license-aware\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":30,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Support configurable language rulesets, tile distributions, and dictionaries\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nMake the phone controller and shared board comfortable for repeated real-world play.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- optimize rack selection and board placement for touch input and small screens\\n- use input-capability detection rather than screen width alone when choosing interaction affordances\\n- provide clear selected, tentative, invalid, waiting, disconnected, and active-turn states\\n- support keyboard interaction on desktop without forcing a keyboard-centric mobile [PROJECT_221]\\n- add accessible names, focus behavior, contrast, and reduced-motion behavior\\n- keep high-frequency controls compact and remove decorative information that competes with gameplay\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- the player flow is usable in portrait and landscape on representative phones\\n- touch targets and [PATH] interactions do not cause stuck selections after pointer cancellation\\n- keyboard-only users can compose and commit a move\\n- automated accessibility checks cover the main board, lobby, join flow, and player controller\\n- browser visual tests protect the compact layouts\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":29,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Polish mobile interaction, responsive layout, and accessibility\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nMake completed and in-progress games inspectable from authoritative replay evidence without creating a second gameplay state store.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- retain a compact turn-level history derived from accepted authoritative commands and resulting scores\\n- expose public move history suitable for the shared board and completed-game summary\\n- integrate with `[PROJECT_158]` replay evidence rather than inventing an independent event log\\n- represent passes and exchanges without exposing private exchanged letters\\n- provide a deterministic [PATH] path that reconstructs the canonical game result\\n- keep history bounded or explicitly persisted according to deployment needs\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- replaying authoritative evidence reconstructs the same final canonical hash\\n- public history shows played words, score deltas, turn order, passes, and exchanges without hidden rack data\\n- history cannot diverge from the committed board state\\n- reconnecting clients can obtain the current public history\\n- tests cover replay across normal moves, blanks, pass, exchange, and game completion\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":28,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Expose deterministic game history and replay\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nVerify that hidden information and authoritative mutations remain protected even when a browser client is modified or malicious.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- audit player-scoped snapshots so no other rack, bag order, or hidden canonical data crosses the connection boundary\\n- validate every gameplay command against authenticated player identity, connection epoch, current turn, and canonical rack ownership\\n- reject forged tile identities, impossible board coordinates, oversized payloads, and stale commands before mutation\\n- ensure public [PATH] connections receive only public state\\n- make logs and diagnostics avoid printing reconnect capabilities or private racks\\n- add negative integration tests that deliberately construct invalid client messages\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- a client cannot play a tile identity it does not own\\n- a client cannot act as another player or inspect another rack through supported protocol surfaces\\n- [PATH] commands fail closed without destabilizing the match\\n- reconnect capability [PATH] preserves fencing\\n- focused tests cover the primary information-disclosure and forged-command cases\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":27,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Harden private-state and untrusted-client boundaries\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nAllow ordinary refreshes and temporary network loss without destroying an in-progress game.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- persist and reuse the existing `[PROJECT_158]` reconnect capability on each player device\\n- reconnect the shared board to the same match as a [PATH] presentation client\\n- restore the player's scoped rack and current turn state from authoritative snapshots\\n- fence replaced connection epochs so an old tab cannot keep controlling the player\\n- handle [PATH] reconnect capabilities with a clear terminal state\\n- ensure an in-progress tentative editor reconciles safely after reconnect\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- refreshing an admitted phone restores the same player identity and rack when the reconnect capability remains valid\\n- a replaced stale connection cannot submit accepted commands\\n- refreshing the shared board does not create a second match\\n- committed state survives client loss because it lives on the authoritative server\\n- reconnect behavior is covered by browser and server integration tests\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":26,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Support reconnect and resume for board and player clients\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nEnd games deterministically and calculate the final standings according to the configured ruleset.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- define the end conditions for an empty bag plus emptied rack and for consecutive scoreless turns\\n- track the scoreless-turn condition through passes, exchanges, and zero-score moves as required by the ruleset\\n- subtract remaining rack values at game end\\n- award the appropriate rack-value transfer when a player goes out\\n- freeze gameplay commands once the game is finished\\n- publish final public scores and [PATH] information without exposing hidden rack history\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- every supported end condition produces one final transition\\n- final rack adjustments are applied exactly once\\n- duplicate commands cannot reopen or re-finish a game\\n- ties are represented explicitly rather than broken arbitrarily\\n- deterministic fixtures cover bag exhaustion and scoreless-turn termination\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":25,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Implement game completion and final scoring\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nValidate every word formed by a move without hard-wiring a proprietary dictionary into the game engine.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- define a deterministic dictionary lookup interface used only after structural placement validation\\n- validate the main word and every cross word produced by a proposed move\\n- return structured invalid-word feedback without mutating canonical state\\n- support a small committed test dictionary for deterministic fixtures\\n- allow a deployment to supply an appropriate licensed or open word list separately\\n- keep dictionary choice part of the [PATH] identity\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- a move commits only when every required word is accepted by the configured dictionary\\n- dictionary lookup order cannot change the resulting game state\\n- test fixtures do not depend on an external network service\\n- changing dictionary configuration changes the explicit ruleset identity\\n- the repository does not silently redistribute a word list whose license does not permit it\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":24,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Add authoritative dictionary validation behind a pluggable word-list boundary\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nAllow the active player to exchange selected rack tiles when the ruleset permits it.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- select one or more rack tile identities for exchange\\n- enforce the configured minimum remaining-bag requirement\\n- return exchanged tiles and draw replacements using deterministic bag semantics\\n- preserve tile conservation and private rack visibility\\n- advance the turn without changing the board or score\\n- reject stale, duplicate, or impossible exchanges atomically\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- an accepted exchange replaces exactly the selected tile count\\n- rejected exchanges leave rack and bag unchanged\\n- exchanged tiles cannot be drawn twice or lost\\n- the main board learns only that the turn advanced, not which letters were exchanged\\n- deterministic fixtures cover near-empty-bag and blank-tile cases\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":23,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Implement tile exchange support\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nAllow the active player to pass without placing tiles.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- add the authoritative pass command to the turn flow\\n- reject pass commands from non-active or stale players\\n- clear any tentative placement before the pass commits\\n- advance the turn without changing board, bag, rack, or score\\n- surface pass as a deliberate phone action with confirmation appropriate to the compact controller\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- a pass advances exactly one turn\\n- board, scores, bag, and racks remain unchanged\\n- [PATH] pass commands cannot advance multiple turns\\n- pending board previews disappear\\n- replay reproduces the pass and resulting active player deterministically\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":22,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Implement pass-turn support\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nProve the complete first multiplayer slice with one shared board and multiple independent player browsers.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- launch the authoritative [PROJECT_189] server and Pages-style browser build in test mode\\n- create a game from the board\\n- join at least two isolated browser contexts through the invite route\\n- start the game and verify private racks\\n- compose and preview a move from the active player's phone context\\n- commit the move and verify board, score, rack replacement, and turn advancement on every client\\n- repeat a move from the next player\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- the test uses isolated browser contexts so private client storage cannot leak between players\\n- the shared board never receives private rack contents\\n- every client converges after each committed move\\n- a non-active player cannot submit an accepted move\\n- the scenario runs deterministically in CI without depending on an external production deployment\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":21,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Add a multi-device end-to-end test for the core playable loop\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nFinish the post-move rack update without exposing private information.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- remove committed tile identities from the player's rack\\n- draw deterministic replacement tiles up to the configured rack size\\n- handle a partially or fully exhausted bag\\n- publish the updated rack only through that player's scoped snapshot\\n- update the public remaining-tile count\\n- keep rack replacement part of the same authoritative turn result\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- tile conservation holds before and after every committed move\\n- only the player who owns the rack receives its contents\\n- bag exhaustion never creates extra tiles or blocks a legal completed move\\n- reconnecting after a move yields the same authoritative rack\\n- the next player's ability to act does not depend on another client's rack [PROJECT_221] update\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":20,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Draw replacement tiles and refresh the active player's private rack\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nComplete the core turn transaction from a player's draft to the next authoritative turn.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- submit the complete proposed placement as an authoritative command\\n- validate placement structure and all resulting words through the rules boundary\\n- calculate the authoritative score\\n- atomically commit board tiles, score changes, and turn advancement\\n- reject invalid or stale commits without partially mutating state\\n- clear the tentative preview as part of the authoritative transition\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- a valid move changes board, score, and active player exactly once\\n- an invalid move leaves canonical board, score, bag, and rack unchanged\\n- [PATH] commit commands cannot score twice\\n- all clients converge on the same committed board and next turn\\n- replaying the accepted command sequence reproduces the same state hash\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":19,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Commit authoritative moves, score them, and advance turns\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nMirror the current turn's in-progress placements on the shared display without treating them as committed game state.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- transmit bounded tentative-placement updates for the active tu…355 tokens truncated…n- [PATH] a tentative tile restores rack availability correctly\\n- invalid drafts explain the structural problem without committing state\\n- the editor consumes authoritative [PATH] updates safely if the turn changes underneath it\",\"labels\":[],\"number\":17,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Implement phone tile selection and tentative placement editing\"},{\"body\":\"## Goal\\n\\nGive each player a compact private controller that clearly shows their letters and whether they can act.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- render the authenticated player's rack from player-scoped snapshots\\n- show tile [PATH] and blank state\\n- show [PATH] turn status and public scores\\n- disable gameplay interaction outside the player's turn\\n- keep the layout usable on narrow portrait screens without opening the on-screen keyboard for normal tile play\\n- update from authoritative snapshots rather than optimistic rack mutation\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- rack contents update after start and after authoritative draws\\n- waiting players cannot initiate placement commands\\n- reconnecting restores the same authoritative rack\\n- the [PROJECT_221] remains usable at common phone widths and orientations\\n…25104 tokens truncated…reat caches as transparent optimizations\n\n- Correctness must not depend on a warm cache; important verification can succeed from a cold cache.\n- Cache identity includes every relevant input and tool version needed to prevent stale results from masquerading as valid work.\n- Invalid or corrupt caches are safely disposable, while agents should reuse valid caches instead of repeatedly downloading or rebuilding the same inputs.\n\n## REP-016 — Separate dependency acquisition from deterministic build work\n\n- [PATH] phases may use the network to acquire declared dependencies and external inputs.\n- Once those inputs are present, compilation, tests, packaging, generation, and deterministic verification do not unexpectedly reach the public network.\n- Hidden downloads are dependency acquisition and should be made explicit rather than buried inside a build step.\n\n## REP-017 — Control ambient environment at deterministic boundaries\n\n- Ordinary interactive development may inherit the user's shell environment.\n- Hermetic tests, generators, builds, packaging, and release steps explicitly control environment values that can alter behavior, such as locale, time zone, proxies, feature flags, credentials, paths, and compiler settings.\n- Unknown ambient variables must not silently change deterministic output.\n\n## REP-018 — Keep build artifacts reproducible from declared inputs\n\n- Artifact contents do not accidentally depend on wall-clock time, host name, user name, absolute checkout path, random build IDs, or other ambient machine state.\n- When build metadata is part of the product contract, derive it from declared reproducible inputs such as the source revision or release metadata.\n\n## REP-019 — Validate configuration against real contracts\n\n- Use official or ecosystem-native schemas and validators when a configuration format has an established contract.\n- Repository-owned machine-readable configuration gains deterministic structural validation once other tooling consumes its shape.\n- Do not invent schemas for incidental data files merely because they use JSON, TOML, or YAML.\n\n## REP-020 — Normalize